  instructions:"Pull a fresh shot of espresso. Fill a Nick & Nora or rocks glass with ice. Pour Licor 43 over the ice first. Slowly pour the hot espresso over the back of a spoon to create a layered effect — the hot espresso will float briefly before integrating. Serve immediately.",
  notes:"The layered pour is essential to the visual drama — hot espresso floating on cold Licor 43 is one of the most beautiful sights in cocktail making. Drink quickly to enjoy the temperature contrast. A double shot of espresso makes it more intense.",
  riffs:"Substitute mezcal for espresso liqueur for a smokier, more Mexican-influenced variation.", tastingNotes:"", lore:"The Carajillo is a traditional Spanish and Latin American coffee drink with roots in the Cuban wars of independence, when Spanish soldiers allegedly mixed coffee with rum for courage — 'coraje' meaning courage. The modern Mexican version, made with Licor 43, became a phenomenon in upscale Mexico City bars in the 2010s before spreading globally. Licor 43 — a Spanish liqueur made from 43 ingredients including vanilla and citrus — was purpose-built for exactly this kind of combination.",
