          type:"Technique",
          isCollection:false,
          showInline:false,
          inStock:false,
          collectionIds:["col-techniques"],
          tags:[],
          yield:"",
          description:"Rolling is the gentle alternative to shaking — a technique that chills and combines without aggression. Instead of sealing a shaker and thrashing the contents with ice, you pour the drink back and forth between two vessels in a slow, controlled stream, letting gravity and movement do the work. Where shaking beats air into a drink and tears delicate ingredients apart, rolling preserves them. Mint leaves emerge intact rather than shredded into bitter flecks. The pour distributes muddled fruit and herbs evenly throughout the glass. Build the drink directly in the glass, place a shaker tin over it, and roll the contents back and forth four or five times. Top with soda, garnish, serve. Best used for: Mojitos, Bloody Marys, and any drink where shaking would damage delicate ingredients. Not for: drinks that need vigorous emulsification or the aeration shaking provides.",
          ingredients:[],
          instructions:"1. Build your drink in the glass. 2. Pour the contents back and forth between the glass and the shaker tin 4 to 5 times in a slow, controlled stream. 3. Listen for a smooth, continuous pour. 4. Return everything to the glass, top with soda, give a gentle stir with the straw. Garnish and serve.",
          notes:"Keep the tins touching lip to lip on your first pours. A broken stream means you are going too fast. The goal is chill and integration, not aeration.",
